module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.GRing
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.DiscreteValuationRing.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.FractionRing
import Sheaf.RingTheory.CompletionOfDVR

/-!
# Regularity of completion maps for characteristic-zero discrete valuation rings

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Proposition Tag 07PX, proof of (3) and (4);
Algebra, Lemma Tag 00PD (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-dvr-completion-regular).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

namespace AdicCompletion

/-- The canonical maximal-ideal-adic completion map of a discrete valuation ring (a Noetherian
local domain of dimension one with principal maximal ideal, Stacks Algebra Tag 00PD; Mathlib's
`IsDiscreteValuationRing`) is regular if its fraction field has characteristic zero.
Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Proposition Tag 07PX, proof of (3) and (4), using
Algebra, Lemma Tag 00PD (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-dvr-completion-regular).

Proof (the node's seven steps):
1. Dimension one (a discrete valuation ring is not a field) forces the maximal ideal to be
   nonzero. Choose a nonzero generator `π`.
   The completion map is flat by `lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-completion-flat` (Tag 00MB).
2. By `lem-weil-pbc-dvr-unit-times-uniformizer-power`, every nonzero element is a unit times
   a power of `π`. Thus every nonzero prime contains `π` and equals the maximal ideal.
   The only primes are zero and the maximal ideal.
3. By `lem-weil-pbc-completion-of-dvr-is-dvr` (Tag 0AP1), the completion is again a DVR.
   By `lem-weil-pbc-local-completion-local-ring`, its maximal ideal is generated by the
   image of `π`, which is nonzero. Apply the unit-times-uniformizer-power lemma there too.
4. The generic fibre is the localization of the completion at the nonzero elements of `A`
   by `lem-weil-pbc-localization-base-change`. This localization is its fraction field:
   the image of `π` is inverted and every nonzero element is a unit times a power of it.
   This fibre is Noetherian by `lem-weil-pbc-field-regular-ring`.
5. Every finite purely inseparable extension of `K` is trivial in characteristic zero
   (`def-weil-pbc-finite-purely-inseparable-extension`). Hence every such base change of
   the generic fibre is still a field, regular by `lem-weil-pbc-field-regular-ring`.
6. By `lem-weil-pbc-tensor-over-quotient-ring` and
   `lem-weil-pbc-local-completion-local-ring`, the closed fibre is the residue field as an
   algebra over itself. Every finite purely inseparable base change is again a field,
   hence regular by `lem-weil-pbc-field-regular-ring`; the closed fibre is Noetherian.
7. Flatness and these two geometrically regular fibres give regularity of the map by
   `def-weil-pbc-g-ring` (Tag 07BZ). -/
theorem isRegular_algebraMap_of_isDiscreteValuationRing_of_charZero
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsDiscreteValuationRing A]
    (K : Type v) [Field K] [Algebra A K] [IsFractionRing A K] [CharZero K] :
    RingHom.IsRegular (algebraMap A (AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) A)) := by
  sorry

end AdicCompletion
